// How memory works: small values on the stack, big ones in the program's arena.
//
// A value under 1 KB lives on the stack, like a local in C. A value of 1 KB
// or more (a big array, a big buffer) lives in the program's arena, so a
// program with one names an arena at the top of its module — the one main
// is in; without one, the compiler stops and says so:
//
//     #allocator Arena_Debug(16 * MB)
//
// It can also go among main's own statements, if that reads better there —
// main's entry makes the arena either way, so code before it runs on it too.
//
// 16 * MB is how much the arena holds in all. It hands out memory by moving
// a cursor forward, and frees by moving it back to a mark. A block holding a
// big value marks the arena when it starts and resets it when it ends, so
// that block's big values are freed with it, without anything to write.
//
// Arena_Debug records every allocation and lists them when it runs out;
// Arena_Basic is the fast one for a finished program. Both are in
// mara.memory.
//
// Part of a program can run on an arena of its own — an arena as a value,
// and a `with` block run on it: see with_arena.mara.
module arenas

use mara.core
use mara.memory

#allocator Arena_Debug(16 * MB)

main :: fun() {
	// 2000 i64s are 16000 bytes: in the arena.
	table : [2000!]i64
	table[0] = 1
	print(table.len, table[0])                    // 2000 1

	// Each round's buffer is freed when the round ends, so going round
	// doesn't use up the arena.
	for round in 0:3 {
		buffer : [4 * KB]byte
		print("round", round, "buffer holds", buffer.cap)
	}
}
